# --- Database ---
# Local: the owner role. Production: the app process gets a wm_helpdesk_app URL (SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE
# on app tables, INSERT/SELECT only on AuditLog); `prisma migrate deploy` runs separately with the
# OWNER url. See prisma/sql/runtime-roles.sql.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres@localhost:5432/helpdesk_dev
# Optional. When set, the client-facing publicDb connects with it. Production: the wm_helpdesk_public
# role (SELECT on ten "Ticket" columns, "ClientVisibleComment" and "ClientVisibleOpeningMessage" ONLY)
# so the client path is read-only at the privilege layer, not just in the Prisma hook.
# Unset = publicDb reuses DATABASE_URL (local dev).
PUBLIC_DATABASE_URL=
# DB-gated tests ONLY (never read by the app). A separate database — immutability triggers make test
# rows permanent, so tests must never run against helpdesk_dev. Create once:
#   createdb wm_helpdesk_test && DATABASE_URL=postgresql://…/wm_helpdesk_test npx prisma migrate deploy
TEST_DATABASE_URL=

# --- Auth.js ---
AUTH_SECRET=replace-with-output-of-openssl-rand-base64-32
# Deployed: the service's own https URL (Cloud Run: Terraform's auth_url, set after the first deploy prints it).
AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000
AUTH_TRUST_HOST=true

# --- Okta OIDC (all three required in production) ---
AUTH_OKTA_ID=
AUTH_OKTA_SECRET=
AUTH_OKTA_ISSUER=https://<tenant>.okta.com/oauth2/default
# Okta side: on this authorization server add a "groups" claim to the ID token, filter
# "Matches regex" ^(ops_team|advisors|client_services|compliance_officers)$, include in "Any scope".
# The app requests scope "openid profile email groups". Okta's built-in `groups` scope exists ONLY on
# the Org authorization server (issuer https://<tenant>.okta.com). On a CUSTOM authorization server
# such as /oauth2/default you must ALSO create a custom scope literally named `groups` (Security >
# API > Authorization Servers > <server> > Scopes) or the token request fails with invalid_scope.
# Either point AUTH_OKTA_ISSUER at the Org AS, or create the scope. Setting the claim's "Include in"
# to "Any scope" makes the claim independent of which choice you make.
#
# Optional (Decision #58). Unset = scope "openid profile email groups", claim "groups". To sign in through the
# SAME Okta application as OPS_PORTAL's dev instance — its authorization server releases the group list as the claim
# "OpsPortalGroups" under the custom scope "ops_portal_groups", and the groups follow OPS_PORTAL's "<APP>-<Role>" names
# ("OPSPORTAL-Operations", "OPSPORTAL-Client Service", "OPSPORTAL-Advisor", "OPSPORTAL-Admin", …), which src/lib/okta-groups.ts maps onto
# the four internal groups (unknown names are dropped; compliance needs a dedicated "OPSPORTAL-Compliance" group) — set:
#   AUTH_OKTA_SCOPE=openid email profile ops_portal_groups
#   AUTH_OKTA_GROUPS_CLAIM=OpsPortalGroups
# A set-but-malformed value (scope without "openid", claim that is not an identifier, or a claim naming a scalar such
# as "email" / "name" / "sub" / "preferred_username") is refused at boot by key name (Decision #59). Only "OPS-PORTAL-*"
# names are eligible for the map; "… Read Only" / "… Viewer" and qualified admins ("OPSPORTAL-Client Service Admin")
# never grant the admin set.
AUTH_OKTA_SCOPE=
AUTH_OKTA_GROUPS_CLAIM=

# --- Google OIDC (optional, domain-restricted) ---
# A second OIDC provider standing in for Okta while the Okta application is an outstanding IT request. It
# ADDS a sign-in method wherever it is set and never replaces Okta: the production invariant above still
# requires the Okta trio where APP_ENV=production. Retiring the whole thing is unsetting the pair.
# The id and the secret are ALL OR NOTHING — one without the other registers no provider at all, so there
# is no half-configured state in which a Google button exists and cannot complete a sign-in.
AUTH_GOOGLE_ID=
AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET=
# Comma-separated domain allowlist. UNSET, EMPTY AND WHITESPACE-ONLY ALL MEAN THE HARDCODED DEFAULT
# "example.com,example.net" — never "every domain", which is the single outcome the feature
# exists to make impossible. A value that IS set but is not a list of bare domains (a scheme, an "@", a
# lone ",,,") is refused at boot by key name, the AUTH_OKTA_SCOPE rule (Decision #58): an operator who
# wrote the variable meant it to take effect, and a silent fall-back is not what they asked for.
# Entries are matched EXACTLY against the domain after the final "@" of a VERIFIED Google address, and
# against the token's `hd` claim, so a subdomain needs listing in its own right.
AUTH_GOOGLE_ALLOWED_DOMAINS=
# Comma-separated internal group values (ops_team, advisors, client_services, compliance_officers) granted
# to a Google sign-in. UNSET = [] = the person authenticates and lands on /no-access (I12). That is correct
# and deliberate — proving you hold a company mailbox is not the same as holding a role on this desk — and
# must not be "fixed" with a default group. A set-but-unknown name is refused at boot by key name.
AUTH_GOOGLE_GROUPS=

# --- Development login (fixed personas). AUTH_DEV_LOGIN=true is honoured only when APP_ENV != production;
# production Terraform hard-sets APP_ENV=production and never declares AUTH_DEV_LOGIN. NODE_ENV plays no part:
# Cloud Run runs the dev service with NODE_ENV=production too, and the personas are its approved sign-in method
# until an Okta application exists (Decision #57). With APP_ENV=production the combination is refused three
# times over: src/instrumentation.ts runs parseEnv + assertDevLoginNotInProduction at `next start` so the
# process exits at boot, src/auth.ts asserts at module load, and assertServingInvariants() re-checks on the
# first request and on every /api/health probe. ---
AUTH_DEV_LOGIN=false

# --- Dev seed fixtures (personas, staff, 37 tickets, synthetic audit rows). Read ONLY by
# `npm run db:seed`; refused (exit 2, nothing written) when APP_ENV=production — the deployment label, not
# NODE_ENV, which is production in the dev migrate job too. Production runs the reference seed only; the dev
# migrate job seeds the fixtures exactly once with SEED_DEV_FIXTURES=true (Decision #57). ---
SEED_DEV_FIXTURES=false

# --- Evidentiary IP capture: number of trusted reverse-proxy hops in front of the app.
# 0 = no proxy (`next dev` locally; audited IP is x-real-ip or "unknown"). Every production BUILD that is
# served — APP_ENV=production, the deployed dev instance, a local `next start` — requires >= 1. Cloud Run: 1,
# because Google's front end appends the connecting client's address as the LAST x-forwarded-for element and
# hops=1 selects exactly that element; a client-forged prefix is never believed (I9). ---
TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=0

# --- Client-facing API (server-to-server bearer token, >= 32 chars). Unset = API disabled (always 401). ---
CLIENT_API_TOKEN=

# ===========================================================================
# Optional integrations. EVERY key below is optional and FAIL-CLOSED: unset means the
# feature it gates is OFF, never "on with a default". Nothing this wave builds can transmit anything
# unless its key is present — that is the mechanism behind "built, not sending". A key that is SET
# but malformed (bad JSON, a non-https webhook, a short secret) is refused at boot BY KEY NAME.
# ===========================================================================

# --- Machine bearer for the ingest, export and outbox routes (>= 32 chars). Mirrors CLIENT_API_TOKEN:
# constant-time compare, a seeded integration User (integration@wm-helpdesk.local) is the actor, and
# every call and every denial is audited. Unset = /api/ingest/*, /api/export/* and
# /api/integrations/* answer 401 to everyone. Generate with: openssl rand -base64 48 ---
INTEGRATION_API_TOKEN=

# --- Per-surface machine bearers (>= 32 chars each), all optional. Each one NARROWS: where it is set,
# INTEGRATION_API_TOKEN no longer opens that surface, so one n8n workflow's credential can be split off
# without rotating all four at once. Unset = that surface falls back to INTEGRATION_API_TOKEN, which is
# the shipped behaviour. A value that is SET but shorter than 32 characters DISABLES its surface (401 to
# everyone) rather than silently falling back to the broad key you were retiring.
#   INGEST_API_TOKEN  -> /api/ingest/*        (phone-system-voicemail-intake, gmail-intake)
#   EXPORT_API_TOKEN  -> /api/export/*        (opsportal-mirror-sync — the whole ticket book, read)
#   OUTBOX_API_TOKEN  -> /api/integrations/*  (outbox-gmail-sender — leases and marks client mail sent)
# See src/lib/integration-scope.ts. ---
INGEST_API_TOKEN=
EXPORT_API_TOKEN=
OUTBOX_API_TOKEN=

# --- HMAC key for the outbound reply token (>= 32 chars). The token travels in the outbound subject
# and Reply-To so an inbound reply can be threaded back to its ticket without trusting the subject
# line. Unset = outbound messages carry no reply token and inbound threading falls back to
# In-Reply-To / References only. Rotating it invalidates tokens already in clients' mailboxes, so
# rotate with an overlap window. Generate with: openssl rand -base64 48 ---
REPLY_TOKEN_SECRET=

# --- Reply-token secrets that are still ACCEPTED but never used to sign: the overlap window above.
# Comma-separated, each held to the same 32-character minimum. Set it to the OLD value at the moment
# you rotate REPLY_TOKEN_SECRET, and remove it once no inbound reply has threaded on it for a couple
# of weeks (`[email-intake] reply token verified by a RETIRED secret` in the logs is the signal).
# Unset = only the current secret verifies, and every token already in a client's mailbox is dead. ---
REPLY_TOKEN_PREVIOUS_SECRETS=

# --- Inbound mailbox map (JSON object). Which queue, brand and default category each support address
# lands in. An address that is NOT in this map is suspended with reason UNKNOWN_MAILBOX and never
# becomes a ticket. Unset = every inbound address is unknown, which is the safe default.
# Keys are lower-cased addresses; "*@domain" and "*" are accepted as fallbacks and an exact match
# wins. A `+tag` suffix on the delivered address is ignored for matching, so the reply-token address
# service+wm.<token>@example.com resolves to the service@ entry.
# Example (single line in the real file):
#   {"service@example.com":{"queueKey":"CLIENT_SERVICE","brand":"CEDAR_RIDGE_WEALTH","category":"OTHER"},
#    "ops@example.com":{"queueKey":"OPERATIONS","brand":"CEDAR_RIDGE_WEALTH"},
#    "help@example.net":{"queueKey":"CLIENT_SERVICE","brand":"CEDAR_RIDGE_DIRECT"}}
MAILBOX_MAP=

# --- Trusted Authentication-Results authserv-ids (comma-separated). The hostname the RECEIVING MTA
# stamps on delivery — Google Workspace writes `mx.google.com`. A verdict carrying any other
# authserv-id is displayed but NEVER authenticates a message, because a sender can write the header
# themselves. Adding an entry is a security decision: it says "this host's word is enough to attribute
# a wire instruction to a client". Unset = the module default (mx.google.com); an empty list is NOT how
# you say "trust nobody" — it would hold every inbound message. Change this the day desk mail stops
# landing in Google Workspace. See src/lib/email/auth-results.ts. ---
EMAIL_TRUSTED_AUTHSERV_IDS=

# --- Attachments. Unset ATTACHMENTS_BUCKET = the local filesystem backend under .local-attachments/
# (gitignored), which is what local development uses. Set it to a private, uniform-access, CMEK
# bucket in the deployed environments. ATTACHMENTS_SIGNER_EMAIL names the service account whose IAM
# signBlob mints the V4 signatures; unset = the metadata server's own identity (correct on Cloud Run).
# A deployed instance ALSO needs the bucket's CORS rule set to the service's own origin, allowing the
# x-goog-content-length-range header on the preflight — otherwise the browser's direct PUT to the
# signed URL fails with an opaque network error (infra/terraform-dev: attachments_cors_origins). ---
ATTACHMENTS_BUCKET=
ATTACHMENTS_SIGNER_EMAIL=

# --- CSP connect-src origin for the attachment upload PUT. Unset = https://storage.googleapis.com,
# which is what the GCS backend signs. Set it to narrow the policy to a private upload host, to a
# comma-separated list, or to the literal "none" to remove the allowance on a deployment that never
# uses object storage. READ AT BUILD TIME (next.config.ts headers() is baked into routes-manifest.json),
# so it must be a build arg, not a Cloud Run runtime variable, to have any effect. It is deliberately
# NOT in src/lib/env.ts: that module is loaded by the running server, and nothing at runtime reads
# this key. See src/lib/security-headers.ts. ---
ATTACHMENTS_UPLOAD_ORIGIN=

# --- Nightly duplicate copy (Rule 204-2(g)): chain-verified audit CSV + a metadata dump, with a
# sha256 manifest, to a retention-locked bucket. Unset = the job logs that it is disabled and exits 0. ---
DUPLICATE_COPY_BUCKET=

# --- Slack. Both must be https. Unset = the jobs still run, still dedupe and still write their audit
# rows; they simply post nothing. That is how this wave stays built-but-silent until the owner approves
# the message text. SLACK_QUEUE_WEBHOOKS is a JSON object
# keyed by QueueKey: {"OPERATIONS":"https://hooks.slack.com/services/...","ADVICE":"https://..."} ---
SLA_ALERT_SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL=
SLACK_QUEUE_WEBHOOKS=

# --- Identity the scheduled-jobs runtime acts as. Unset = the seeded jobs@wm-helpdesk.local row. ---
JOB_ACTOR_EMAIL=

# --- Absolute https origin of the staff app, used for the deep links in Slack alerts. Unset falls
# back to AUTH_URL, and with neither set a link degrades to a bare /tickets/wm/<number> path. ---
APP_BASE_URL=

# --- Deployment label: local | dev | demo | production. Drives the topbar banner AND the environment classification
# (Decision #57): the production-only rules (Okta trio required, dev login forbidden) key on
# APP_ENV=production whatever NODE_ENV says; a production build served under dev or local needs AUTH_SECRET,
# DATABASE_URL, TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS >= 1 and at least one sign-in method (the Okta trio, the temporary Google
# pair AUTH_GOOGLE_ID + AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET, or AUTH_DEV_LOGIN=true).
# Terraform sets it explicitly per service (dev → dev, production → production); unset = local. The public
# portfolio demo runs as `demo`, treated like dev with its own banner. Any other value is refused at boot by
# key name. ---
APP_ENV=local

# ===========================================================================
# More optional integrations
#
# Same rule as Wave A, without exception: every key below is OPTIONAL and FAIL-CLOSED. Unset DISABLES
# the feature it gates; it never enables one with a default. A key that is SET but malformed is refused
# at boot BY KEY NAME — values are never echoed. Keys that are only meaningful TOGETHER are refused as
# a group when half of them are set, because a half-configured feature looks configured and silently
# is not: a VAPID public key with no private key means every browser subscribes and nothing is ever
# delivered, and an AI provider with no project means the draft button appears and fails on click.
#
# NOTHING HERE IS SET ANYWHERE. That is the acceptance criterion for the wave, not a style rule.
# ===========================================================================

# --- Slack direct messages for notifications (B1). A JSON OBJECT mapping a staff email to a Slack
# member id: {"mia.ops@wm-helpdesk.local":"U01ABCDEF"}. Unset = no Slack DM is ever attempted and the
# in-app inbox is the only channel. It is a map rather than a lookup because the desk's Slack is a
# different directory from Okta and the two do not agree about addresses. ---
SLACK_USER_MAP=

# --- Web push (B10; RFC 8291 encryption, RFC 8292 VAPID). ALL THREE OR NONE.
# VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY / VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY: base64url, generated by `npm run pwa:vapid` (which prints them
# and writes nothing). The PUBLIC key is handed to every browser that subscribes — that is what it is
# for — and the private key signs the JWT the push service checks.
# VAPID_SUBJECT: a `mailto:` or `https:` URI identifying the operator, e.g. mailto:ops@example.com.
# Unset = POST /api/push/subscribe answers 404, the opt-in control renders nothing at all, and
# `sendWebPush` returns "disabled". ---
VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
VAPID_SUBJECT=

# --- AI reply drafting (B3). `vertex` or `demo`; anything else is refused by key name. `vertex` REQUIRES
# the three VERTEX_* values. `demo` (src/lib/ai/demo.ts) returns a canned draft per category and drafts only
# in demo mode. Authentication is Application
# Default Credentials on the Cloud Run service account — there is no API key here and there must not
# be one. Unset = the draft button is hidden and the seam is inert.
# A draft is never auto-sent: it fills the composer, a human edits it, and a human submits it. The
# AI_DRAFT_REQUESTED audit row carries a HASH of the draft, never its text. ---
AI_DRAFTING_PROVIDER=
VERTEX_PROJECT=
VERTEX_LOCATION=
VERTEX_MODEL=

# --- Scheduled-job policy (B4). AUTO_CLOSE_DAYS: how many days a RESOLVED ticket waits before the
# auto-close job closes it (positive integer; unset = the job logs "disabled" and exits 0).
# DIGEST_HOUR_LOCAL: hour of the FIRM day (0..23, America/New_York) the notification digest posts;
# unset = 8. Neither ever acts on a ticket a human touched in the meantime. ---
AUTO_CLOSE_DAYS=
DIGEST_HOUR_LOCAL=

# --- SYSTEM-authored client messages (B5). "true" enables; anything else, including unset, disables.
# AUTO_ACK_ENABLED: an acknowledgement on a newly ingested ticket.
# RESOLVED_NOTICE_ENABLED: a notice when a ticket reaches RESOLVED.
# Both are authored as SYSTEM (never a named staff member), NEVER stamp firstRespondedAt — an automatic
# receipt is not a first response, and counting it as one would make every SLA number a lie — and both
# respect RequesterSuppression. The wording needs the owner's approval before either is switched on. ---
AUTO_ACK_ENABLED=
RESOLVED_NOTICE_ENABLED=

# --- Outbound webhooks (B5). "true" arms the dispatcher. Unset = endpoints can be configured at
# /admin/webhooks and NOTHING is ever sent, which is how the surface is reviewable before it transmits.
# Each endpoint carries its own signing secret, shown once at creation. Payloads are PII-light by
# contract: ids, enum values and counts — never a subject, a body or a requester address. ---
WEBHOOKS_ENABLED=

# The app-held half of every webhook signing key. At least 32 characters; hold it in Secret Manager and
# NEVER in the database. An endpoint row stores only a random per-endpoint salt, and the key a receiver
# verifies with is HMAC-SHA256(WEBHOOK_SIGNING_KEY, "wmhd:webhook-signing:v1:<salt>") — so a dump, a
# backup or a restore drill of the database alone yields nothing that can forge a signature. Unset = no
# endpoint can be created and the dispatcher signs nothing (it records the delivery as failed instead of
# sending it unsigned). Rotating it invalidates every existing endpoint's secret: re-create the
# endpoints, or keep the old value until each receiver has been re-keyed. ---
WEBHOOK_SIGNING_KEY=

# --- MCP server (B8). "true" serves POST /api/mcp. Unset = the route answers 404 to everyone, bearer
# or not. A caller still needs a ClientApiToken carrying the `mcp` scope; every tool call is audited
# with `metadata.via = "mcp"`, because an agent acting on a person's behalf is a different fact from
# a workflow posting. ---
MCP_ENABLED=

# --- Dev-only requester-domain guard (B11). Comma-separated domains an incoming requester address may
# use; a leading dot is accepted and ignored. When SET, an address outside the list cannot become a
# ticket at all — which is what stops a dev instance full of realistic fixtures from ever acquiring a
# real client's address. Production leaves it UNSET on purpose: production's requester domains are every
# domain Cedar Ridge's clients use, which is not a list anyone can maintain, and a guard that has to be
# disabled to work is a guard that will be disabled. Terraform sets it on dev only. ---
ALLOWED_REQUESTER_DOMAINS=

# --- Legacy help desk archive (one-off import tooling). Read-only API access to the help desk this one
# replaced; unset everywhere except the machine that runs the import. ---
LEGACY_DESK_SUBDOMAIN=
LEGACY_DESK_EMAIL=
LEGACY_DESK_API_TOKEN=

# --- Portfolio demo (src/lib/demo/). NEXT_PUBLIC_DEMO_MODE=on turns on the stand-ins for the mail sender
# and Slack, the Outbox screen, the demo AI provider, the upload refusal and the persona-only sign-in. Read
# at build time as well as run time; refused wherever APP_ENV=production. The demo also sets APP_ENV=demo,
# AUTH_DEV_LOGIN=true, AI_DRAFTING_PROVIDER=demo, EMAIL_TRUSTED_AUTHSERV_IDS=mx.example.com, a MAILBOX_MAP
# for service@example.com, the two Slack addresses pointing at the stand-in
# (http://127.0.0.1:<STAND_INS_PORT>/slack/<channel>/<secret>), and fresh OUTBOX_API_TOKEN, INGEST_API_TOKEN
# and REPLY_TOKEN_SECRET each time the container starts. ---
NEXT_PUBLIC_DEMO_MODE=
# Loopback port of the Slack stand-in inside the container (default 8090).
STAND_INS_PORT=
# The portfolio site the demo links back to.
NEXT_PUBLIC_PORTFOLIO_URL=
# Where the seed and the sample emails read the synthetic universe; default ../../synthetic/out/universe.json.
DEMO_UNIVERSE_PATH=
